gcr-ssh-agent: route security key prompts through GCR

gcr-ssh-agent spawns OpenSSH's ssh-agent with no askpass of its own, so
when that ssh-agent signs with a FIDO ("sk") key it asks for the PIN and
shows the "Confirm user presence" request through whatever SSH_ASKPASS is
in the environment, or /usr/libexec/ssh-askpass, which GNOME systems
usually don't have. The PIN prompt fails ("incorrect passphrase supplied
to decrypt private key", agent refused operation) and the touch request is
never shown (gcr#116, gcr#136). gcr4-ssh-askpass also ignores
SSH_ASKPASS_PROMPT, so a confirmation for an `ssh-add -c` key, or a touch
notification, turns into a password prompt, and the latter is left behind
when OpenSSH kills the notifier.

Based on gcr!173 ("ssh-agent: route askpass prompts through GCR"), with
two differences:

- Only the gcr-ssh-agent and gcr4-ssh-askpass executables change; libgcr
  (whose GcrSshAskpass gcr-ssh-agent links to) is left as it is. The
  ssh-agent child gets its askpass through the public
  gcr_ssh_askpass_child_setup() plus SSH_ASKPASS_REQUIRE=force, and a
  confirmation is told apart by a prefix on the relayed message, which
  the library passes through untouched.
- The touch request is shown rather than dropped: with
  SSH_ASKPASS_PROMPT=none, gcr4-ssh-askpass opens a system prompt itself
  and waits. When OpenSSH kills it after the touch, the prompter closes
  the dialog because the caller left the bus.

The security key PIN and the confirmation use an interaction with no
keyring fields, so nothing is looked up in or stored to the login keyring.
The askpass also joined its arguments with a leading space instead of
between them.

